When there's a Shiny plot with click and dblclick listeners, those initial reactive values are `NULL` (since there are no interactions on the initial plot render). If that plot is then updated, the click and dblclick reactive values are invalidated (despite still being `NULL`). These invalidations happens on the _first plot update **only**_ ... subsequent plot updates don't invalidate.

Below is a simple app demonstrating the issue (also visible running [here](https://murman.shinyapps.io/interactive-plot-events/)). To observe, refresh the plot **without clicking on the plot**. Note that the initial invalidation counts are all 1, then click and dblclick are incremented to 2 on the first plot refresh, then they stay at 2 for subsequent plot refreshes.

I think there should be no invalidations in this case (unless the dblclick or click values are non-NULL and the plot has been updated, in which case setting those back to NULL (and invalidating) for the new plot makes sense). A relatively easy hackfix is to 'buffer' the `_dblclick` and `_click` reactive values by observing them and setting a proxy `reactiveVal()`, as that'll only invalidate if the actual value changes, but this was tricky for me to track down since I have an app that acts on double-click invalidation that was ... well ... acting when it shouldn't :-)

```R
library(tibble)
library(shiny)

ui <- fluidPage(
  plotOutput("tblplot", click = "tblplot_click", dblclick = "tblplot_dblclick", brush = brushOpts("tblplot_brush", resetOnNew = TRUE)),
  actionButton("refresh_plot", "Refresh plot"),
  verbatimTextOutput("counts")
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {

  f_tbl <- function() tibble(x = rnorm(100), y = rnorm(100))

  output$tblplot <- renderPlot({
    input$refresh_plot
    plot(f_tbl())
  })

  invalidate_counts <- reactiveValues(
    brush = 0,
    click = 0,
    dblclick = 0
  )

  observe({
    input$tblplot_click
    isolate(invalidate_counts$click <- invalidate_counts$click + 1)
  })
  observe({
    input$tblplot_dblclick
    isolate(invalidate_counts$dblclick <- invalidate_counts$dblclick + 1)
  })
  observe({
    input$tblplot_brush
    isolate(invalidate_counts$brush <- invalidate_counts$brush + 1)
  })

  output$counts <- renderPrint({
    reactiveValuesToList(invalidate_counts)
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```

First described here: https://github.com/r-lib/R6/issues/232.

If one creates an observer from within an R6 object, then tries to `destroy()` that observer via the object's `finalize()` method, the R6 object will never be `gc()`-ed under normal circumstances. Instead, one must _explicitly_ `destroy()` the observer, and then only after a reactive flush will the R6 object (and its encapsulated observer) finally be `gc()`-ed.

The pattern I'd initially hoped would work was to place the `destroy()` method call inside the encapsulator's `finalize()` method, thus cleaning-up the observer field during release of the R6 object. This pattern doesn't work at the moment, however, as there's effectively a circular reference path between the observer and the R6 object, each preventing the other from being fully destroyed. Here's the original pattern idea:
```r
WithObs <- R6::R6Class("WithObs",
  private = list(
    obs = NULL,
    finalize = function() {
      print(sprintf("%s: running finalize()", class(self)[1]))
      private$obs$destroy()
    }
  ),
  public = list(
    initialize = function(rx) {
      private$obs <- shiny::observe(print(rx()))
    }
  )
)
```
If we try something like so:
```r
rxval <- reactiveVal(0)
with_obs <- WithObs$new(rxval)
rm(with_obs)
gc()
```
... `with_obs`'s `finalize()` is never run and thus neither is the observer's `destroy()` method. And since the main session has now lost the `with_obs` reference, it's awfully hard to avoid a memleak of sorts here (save for some other pretty tricky Shiny plumbing to 'recover' a reference to the registered observer).

If, on the other hand, we now add a (public) method to the `WithObs` class:
```r
# ... in the public list(...)
free = function() {
  private$obs$destroy()
}
```
... and use the C/C++ [alloc/free](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_dynamic_memory_allocation)-style pattern (or, perhaps more aptly, R's `UNPROTECT` system):
```r
rxval <- reactiveVal(0)
with_obs <- WithObs$new(rxval)  ## effectively an alloc
with_obs$free()  ## destroy the observer
rm(with_obs)
gc()  ## no finalize() yet ...
shiny:::flushReact()
gc()  ## now finalize() (finally) runs
```

I don't see an immediately-obvious solution to this problem (apart from the alloc/free pattern), but I do think it's often useful to encapsulate observers in _some_ type of object to abstract-away some of the reactive implementation details (especially for reusable components in applications). And R6 objects seem like the right sort of encapsulator specifically _because_ of the ability to register finalizers :-)

