A fairly common Shiny pattern I've seen is to let errors/conditions bubble through reactive conductors and (selectively) handle them in observers & output renderers. Renderers are particularly tolerant of upstream errors (by wrapping using `shiny.silent.error`). So, e.g., this Shiny app won't quit on the 1/10 chance an error is thrown (the renderer instead simply displays the error message):
```r
library(tidyverse)
library(shiny)

ui <- fluidPage(
  textOutput("txt"),
  actionButton("btn", "click me")
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {

  btn_x_10 <- reactive({
    val <- input$btn * 10
    if (runif(1) < 0.1) stop("boom")
    val
  }) # %>% throttle(2000)

  output$txt <- renderText({
    sprintf("%s %s", Sys.time(), btn_x_10())
  })
}

runApp(shinyApp(ui, server), port = 6969)
```
But(!), if we un-comment `%>% throttle(2000)` to slow down the reactive conductor's invalidation signals, the application will quit on the first "boom" as now the error condition inside the observer is unhandled. Adding a `tryCatch` block to the renderer won't help.

The only fix -- as far as I can tell -- would be to add error-handling into the upstream reactive conductor, but this starts to feel like an anti-pattern, since error-handlers should typically be close to the 'application code', and in some cases a programmer may not have access to the upstream reactive. One _could_ always wrap any upstream reactives with error-handlers and pass that wrapped value into `throttle`, but this still feels close to that anti-pattern.

`throttle`, `debounce` (and any other function following the 'wrapped observer sets a reactive value to be returned' pattern) seem to come with this fairly large 'gotcha' about which I haven't seen much written. I wonder if it'd be possible to have the wrapped observers safely try executing, catching any errors along the way. Those errors can be saved and perhaps re-raised when the surrogate reactive value is accessed. Similar in spirit to this psuedocode:
```r
wrap <- function(r) {
  error <- NULL
  errcount <- reactiveVal(0)
  upstream_val <- reactiveVal(NULL)

  observe({
    tryCatch({
      upstream_val(r()) # take upstream dependency; set surrogate reactive val
      error <<- NULL
    }, error = function(e) {
      error <<- e
      errcount(isolate(errcount() + 1)) # increment to force downstream invalidation
    })
  })

  ## returned closure
  function() {
    # (re)take dependencies **before** possibly throwing
    upstream_val()
    errcount()
    if (is.null(error)) stop(error) else upstream_val
  }
}
```
Has anything like this been discussed (or implemented) within Shiny itself?

It is possible to opt out from the loadSupport default behaviour, e.g. when inside a package, by including a file _disable_autoload.r. However, even after including this file, the warning "Loading R/ subdirectory for Shiny application, but this directory appears to contain an R package. Sourcing files in R/ may cause unexpected behavior." appears. I suggest removing this warning. It causes issues e.g. when one wants to upgrade warnings to errors for debugging purposes. One solution could be to issue the warning further down in the function, like so:

```
loadSupport <- function (appDir = NULL, renv = new.env(parent = globalenv()), 
    globalrenv = globalenv()) 
{
    require(shiny)
    if (is.null(appDir)) {
        appDir <- findEnclosingApp(".")
    }
    descFile <- file.path.ci(appDir, "DESCRIPTION")
    if (!is.null(globalrenv)) {
        globalPath <- file.path.ci(appDir, "global.R")
        if (file.exists(globalPath)) {
            withr::with_dir(appDir, {
                sourceUTF8(basename(globalPath), envir = globalrenv)
            })
        }
    }
    helpersDir <- file.path(appDir, "R")
    disabled <- list.files(helpersDir, pattern = "^_disable_autoload\\.r$", 
        recursive = FALSE, ignore.case = TRUE)
    if (length(disabled) > 0) {
        return(invisible(renv))
    }
    if (file.exists(file.path.ci(appDir, "NAMESPACE")) || (file.exists(descFile) && 
        identical(as.character(read.dcf(descFile, fields = "Type")), 
            "Package"))) {
        warning("Loading R/ subdirectory for Shiny application, but this directory appears ", 
            "to contain an R package. Sourcing files in R/ may cause unexpected behavior.")
    }
    helpers <- list.files(helpersDir, pattern = "\\.[rR]$", recursive = FALSE, 
        full.names = TRUE)
    helpers <- sort_c(helpers)
    helpers <- normalizePath(helpers)
    withr::with_dir(appDir, {
        lapply(helpers, sourceUTF8, envir = renv)
    })
    invisible(renv)
}
```

