This example exits on session start if the bug is reproduced. It tracks the order of execution of outputs `one`, `two`, and `three`.

Because `two` has priority > 0, the expected outcome is `two`, `one`, `three`. But the actual outcome is `one`, `two`, `three`.

If you modify the app to get the outputs to all invalidate again (e.g. based on an actionButton click) then you'd get the correct order; the priority is only ignored on the _first_ flush cycle for an output.

```r
library(shiny)

