During my struggle to fix up my app's UI, I have modified a few of the UI functions to provide a more responsive page design. I thought it would be useful in general, so here it goes.

I've learned, largely from [Bootstrap documentation](https://getbootstrap.com/docs/3.3/css/#grid), that different grid sizes makes components break to a new line at different screen width. This can be used to finely control the placement of widgets depending on screen size. Here's my modification to `column`, `sidebarPanel` and `mainPanel`:

```r
# global.R

require(shiny)

# patching column to use xs grid
column <- function (width, ..., grid = 'sm', offset = 0) {
  if (!is.numeric(width) || (width < 1) || (width > 12))
    stop("column width must be between 1 and 12")
  colClass <- paste0("col-", grid, "-", width, collapse = ' ')
  if (offset > 0)
    colClass <- paste0(
      colClass,
      paste0(" col-", grid, "-offset-", offset, collapse = ' ')
    )
  div(class = colClass, ...)
}

# patching sidebarPanel and mainPanel
sidebarPanel <- function (..., grid = 'sm', width = 4) {
  div(class = paste0("col-", grid, "-", width, collapse = ' '), tags$form(class = "well",
    ...))
}

mainPanel <- function (..., grid = 'sm', width = 8) {
  div(class = paste0("col-", grid, "-", width, collapse = ' '), ...)
}
```

With these modified UI functions, I can make an app where two widgets stay on the same line even on the narrowest screen; or make the sidebar panel appear not too squashed when the screen is narrow but not enough to cause stacking. 

