I think the easiest way to illustrate this is with a mock up. So basically, we'd start with a `canvasOutput` slot, that would appear to just be a huge drop target:

![canvas-start](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6527540/27262851-0ecda17a-5457-11e7-8228-6bd163725ca2.png)

The user could then drag a file onto it (with some kind of visual transition):

![canvas-droppish](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6527540/27262901-400a6308-5458-11e7-8e17-05f9c61ad8f9.png)

So, the "cool" part would be that the app author has server logic to specify immediately what to do with that data (is it transformed and visualized through a table? does it generate a plot? etc). And this result would then be rendered onto the same slot (in this case, the data is assumed to be a 2-column table, which is automatically rendered into a ggplot plot):

![canvas-finish](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6527540/27262853-16cbf07a-5457-11e7-904b-44c55de556f6.png)

Here is the mock up code that could go along with that:

```r
library(shiny)
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

ui <- fluidPage(
  titlePanel("canvasOutput mock-up"),
  canvasOutput("plt", height = 500, width = 700)
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {
  uploadedData <- reactiveVal()
  
  output$plt <- renderCanvas({
    onWait = function() {
      "Drop file here"
    },
    onUpload = function(data) {
      # saves the uploaded data in a reactiveVal() so that it's 
      # available outside of this scope
      uploadedData(data)
      vars <- names(data)
      x <- vars[1]
      y <- vars[2]
      
      ggplot(data, aes_string(x, y) ) +
        geom_point() + geom_smooth()
    }
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```

