The code example below demonstrate the problem. At first, the event listener of the Process button is only triggered when the button is clicked on. But, when the button is removed (click on Remove It) and reinstated (then click Replace It), the `observeEvent` listener for `doButton` is fired without it ever being clicked on.

So, there is a problem when `removeUI` is used on a widget and `insertUI` is used some time later with a recycled widget ID. Using a new widget ID which is has not previously been present and removed avoids the problem occurring. Aren't fired events 'consumed' once all of their handlers have completed executing ? Should the current behaviour be changed ?

The reason why the observer is destroyed rather than suspended is because the listener's code is different for the restored Process button.

```
library(shiny)
observerList <- list()    

ui = fluidPage(
  actionButton("doButton", "Process"),
  actionButton("removeButton", "Remove It"),
  actionButton("restoreButton", "Replace It")
)

server = function(input, output)
{
  observerList <<- c(observerList, observeEvent(input[["doButton"]], {
    print("Processing ...")
  }))

  observeEvent(input[["removeButton"]], {
    observerList[[1]][["destroy"]]()
    observerList <<- list()
    removeUI("#doButton")
  })

  observeEvent(input[["restoreButton"]], {
    # Restore button.
    insertUI("#removeButton", where = "beforeBegin", ui = actionButton("doButton", "Process"))

    # Add new event.
    observerList <<- c(observerList, observeEvent(input[["doButton"]], {
    print("Oops!")
    # Doesn't wait for user mouse click.
  }))
  })      
}

shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)
```

