If there's an error in a reactive, it stops a flush from running any remaining observers. Those observers can get triggered with another `flushReact`:

``` R
vals <- reactiveValues(x = 1)

r <- reactive({
  if (vals$x == 0) stop("x is zero!")
  else vals$x
})

observe({
  print("obs1")
  print(r())
})
observe({
  print("obs2")
  print(r())
})

shiny:::flushReact()
# [1] "obs1"
# [1] 1
# [1] "obs2"
# [1] 1


# After an error in the reactive, we need another flush to clear the next
# observer
vals$x <- 0; shiny:::flushReact()
# [1] "obs1"
#  Error in .func() : x is zero!

shiny:::flushReact()
# [1] "obs2"
#  Error in .func() : x is zero!
```

This is the root cause of rstudio/ggvis#102.

I believe the problem is in the use of `withCallingHandlers` here:
https://github.com/rstudio/shiny/blob/be4eff413d71aec82b59ae8cc45c2c4b4ef92244/R/react.R#L66

The function exits as soon as one of the runs of the `lapply`ed function throws an error; in other words, the `withCallingHandlers` doesn't seem to prevent the error from propagating.

``` R
lapply(0:1, function(x) {
  withCallingHandlers({
    if (x == 0) stop("x is zero")
    else print(paste("x is", x))

  }, warning = function(e) {
    print(paste0("warning for x=", x))

  }, error = function(e) {
    print(paste0("error for x=", x))
  })
})
# [1] "error for x=0"
